  <h2>Seoul After Dark</h2>
  <p>Seoul has one of Asia's most vibrant nightlife scenes — and it runs deep into the morning. Hongdae clubs don't get going until midnight. Norebang (karaoke) rooms stay open 24 hours. Pojangmacha street tents serve soju and fried chicken until dawn. The city genuinely never sleeps.</p>

  <h2>🎵 Hongdae — Korea's Club District</h2>
  <p>Hongik University area is the epicenter of Seoul's nightlife. Dense clusters of clubs, live music venues, craft beer bars, and street performers pack into a few city blocks. The energy builds from 10PM and peaks around 2–4AM.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cakeshop</strong> — Underground electronic / house. Intimate, dark, serious music.</li>
    <li><strong>NB2 (New Born 2)</strong> — Hip-hop, R&B. Very popular with locals and foreigners alike.</li>
    <li><strong>Soap</strong> — Techno and electronic. Smaller, cooler crowd.</li>
    <li><strong>FF</strong> — Queer-friendly, diverse music policy.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Cover charge: ₩10,000–₩20,000 usually includes one drink. Dress smart casual — no flip-flops.</p>

  <h2>🌍 Itaewon — International Bars</h2>
  <p>Itaewon's bar scene is the most diverse in Seoul. Craft beer, cocktail lounges, sports bars, dive bars — all within walking distance. The Gyeongridan-gil (Gyeridan) area nearby is more local and hip, with excellent cocktail bars and wine spots.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Craftworks Taphouse</strong> — Best craft beer in Seoul. 30+ taps, Korean and international.</li>
    <li><strong>Haenyeo Bar</strong> — Natural wine, excellent charcuterie.</li>
    <li><strong>Take Urban</strong> — Rooftop bar with N Seoul Tower views.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>💎 Gangnam — Upscale Clubs & Lounges</h2>
  <p>If you want glam, Gangnam delivers. Apgujeong and Cheongdam are full of luxury lounges, celebrity-frequented venues, and the kind of nightclubs that appear on "world's best" lists.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Octagon</strong> — Repeatedly ranked among the world's top 10 clubs. Massive, world-class DJs, strict dress code.</li>
    <li><strong>Club Arena</strong> — Multi-room megaclub. Electronic and hip-hop floors.</li>
    <li><strong>Lounge H</strong> — Upscale cocktail bar, hotel-adjacent.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>🎤 Norebang — Must-Do</h2>
  <p>No Seoul trip is complete without norebang. Unlike Japanese karaoke bars, Korean norebang gives your group a private room — soundproofed, with a full song catalog (thousands of English songs available), tambourines, and a button to order snacks and drinks from outside. Sing until you lose your voice. Prices: ₩10,000–₩20,000 per person per hour.</p>
  <p><strong>Find them:</strong> Coin norebang (cheaper, no time limit per song), regular norebang, luxury norebang. Every neighborhood has them.</p>

  <h2>🏮 Pojangmacha — Street Tent Bars</h2>
  <p>The most authentic late-night Seoul experience. An orange tent, plastic stools, fried snacks, and cheap soju. Pull up a stool, order somaek (soju + beer), and watch Seoul go by until 3AM. Found near major subway exits, Han River, and university areas.</p>

  <div class="tip-box">
    💡 <strong>The Seoul strategy:</strong> Last subway is ~midnight. Cheapest option: stay out until 5:30AM when the first trains run. This is a completely normal Seoul move.
  </div>

  <h2>Getting Home Late</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Night Owl Bus (올빼미버스)</strong> — Runs 12AM–5AM. Look for bus numbers starting with N. Cheap (₩2,150) but limited routes.</li>
    <li><strong>Kakao T Taxi</strong> — App-based, safe, easy. Late-night surcharge of 20% applies midnight–4AM.</li>
    <li><strong>Jungbu (call taxi)</strong> — Ask any convenience store to call one. Speak your destination clearly.</li>
  </ul>
